m the customer down, you need to demonstrate the ability and the willingness to listen calmly to what the customer has to say without interrupting or interjecting your views.", "id": 1}, {"time": "43000", "image": "engenn11le05ob03re2img02.jpg ", "text": "If your customer perceives that you are not attuned to their emotional needs or thinks that you are not working in their best interest, you will become part of the problem, rather than part of the solution.", "id": 2}, {"time": "53000", "image": "engenn11le05ob03re2img02.jpg ", "text": "Keep in mind that a customer generally wants to be respected and acknowledged\nas an individual and as being important. As you interact with the customer, you can soften the situation and reduce emotion by providing customer-focused responses.", "id": 3}, {"time": "68500", "image": "engenn11le05ob03re2img03.jpg ", "text": "Assume a customer has a problem. As the customer approaches, greet them with a smile, open body language and gestures. Then as the customer explains the problem : \u201cI will not be spoken to...\u201d  You can offer statements such as, \u201cI see,\u201d \u201cI will do everything in my power \u2026 \u201d or \u201cI understand how that can feel .\u201d Such statements help you connect with the customer. Once the problem has been defined and resolved, take one more opportunity at the end of your interaction to send a customer-focused message by smiling and thanking the customer for allowing you to assist. Ask if there is anything else you can do, \u201cWould you like to/Would you like me to\u2026?\u201d Also, one last apology may be appropriate for inconvenience, frustration, mistreatment, and so on.", "id": 4}], "audiosrc": "engenn11le05ob03re2aud01", "coverimg": "engenn11le05ob03re2img01.jpg "}}], "id": 1}]}]}